#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106626} :activity/id #uuid "f530f18b-11b2-5bde-8332-6af2fe3c20eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050362 :topic/id #uuid "421dabda-4549-5ad6-b909-e9e0b37e1c41" :topic/math-academy-id 1286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107080 :tutorial/content "The **scalar triple product** of three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is defined as $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFor example, suppose we have three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 1,1,-2 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle 0,3,1 \\rangle.$ Let's compute their scalar triple product, $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFirst, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =7i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨7,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The scalar triple product takes its name from the fact that it multiplies three vectors together and always results in a scalar.\n\nLater, we will learn some geometric intuition behind the scalar triple product. But for now, we will focus on computing the scalar triple product." :tutorial/id #uuid "28eff4c1-2c88-5799-b15d-0ab9f27be09b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2952 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "54962f85-eb4f-5689-8e98-692b298d15d1" :step/math-academy-id 15857 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105741 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105599 :question/id #uuid "2f380683-0d93-541d-a07d-95a1d889c761" :question/math-academy-id "e-4347"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ba6b6b5-ac1d-5a3a-876a-af51fdf50e0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049138}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using the Definition"} :step/id #uuid "3a87ffcc-ec7d-55bd-9fb9-bf8d091686aa" :step/math-academy-id 15858 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107081 :tutorial/content "It turns out that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nThat is to say, if $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's focus on computing the scalar triple product using the determinant above." :tutorial/id #uuid "67915540-ba40-5a4a-a7b0-6d81497d64c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2953 :tutorial/title "The Definition of the Scalar Triple Product Via the Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "49837287-74a9-55df-aeef-eb0b8fd6f361" :step/math-academy-id 15859 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105600 :question/id #uuid "b5cf98f2-087d-580c-a2f6-c37ad2af4c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4348"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408104}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "8fd2fcb8-9be2-5a30-a126-347cf62d3bd6" :step/math-academy-id 15860 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107082 :tutorial/content "The scalar triple product has two important properties.\n\nFirst, scalars can be factored out of the product. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{a}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot ({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{c}) = {\\color{red}2}\\left[ \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\right].\n$$\n\nSecond, if we swap exactly two vectors in the scalar triple product, the result changes sign. For example, swapping $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\cdot ( {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = - {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\cdot ( {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n$$\n\nNote that if we swap twice, then the result remains the same, because the negatives cancel out. For example, swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}$ and then swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-b\\cdot (a\\times c) \\\\  & =-(-b\\cdot (c\\times a)) \\\\  & =b\\cdot (c\\times a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese properties arise naturally from the fact that the scalar triple product can be computed as a determinant.\n\n- If we multiply a row of the matrix by a number, then the determinant is multiplied by that number:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1} & 2{a}_{2} & 2{a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\n- If we swap two rows, then the determinant changes sign:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41cd1550-dd91-5029-a397-fa1417d1e160" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2954 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Scalar Triple Product"} :step/id #uuid "29a88567-7fa3-590d-9466-ca0095d38194" :step/math-academy-id 15861 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105601 :question/id #uuid "483b758a-9b14-5a72-87e5-70f27a0acf48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4349"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377085}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"} :step/id #uuid "06a0d838-fa82-5fdb-bf4d-ea928e28bcaa" :step/math-academy-id 15862 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107083 :tutorial/content "We have been using the fact for three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ the scalar triple product can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut why is this true? To understand where this result comes from, first notice that in the formula for the triple product, the cross product can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a},$ we get an expression that is just the cofactor expansion across the first row of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\cdot (|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k) \\\\  & ={a}_{1}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|-{a}_{2}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|+{a}_{3}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "99f752d5-13c8-5b3e-aeee-383770b0db33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2955 :tutorial/title "A Proof of the Formula for the Scalar Triple Product"} :step/id #uuid "4b903237-363d-5bc1-826b-fb982e1c9d41" :step/math-academy-id 15863}] :activity/title "The Scalar Triple Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105969}]}
